12. GEOGRAPHY MODEL

The application is designed for Algeria.

Places can represent:

wilaya
commune
station
place
custom

Create:

places

Fields:

id
name
type
wilaya
commune
location
is_custom
created_by
created_at

Use:

GEOGRAPHY(POINT, 4326)

for geographic coordinates.

---

13. CUSTOM MAP POINTS

A user may select a point directly on the map.

A checkpoint does not have to exist in the global places database.

Therefore:

trip_checkpoints.place_id

is nullable.

Every checkpoint must still have:

name
location
sequence

---

14. TRIP MODEL

A trip belongs to:

driver
vehicle

Trip fields:

id
driver_id
vehicle_id
departure_at
status
booking_mode
published_at
started_at
completed_at
cancelled_at
created_at
updated_at

Booking mode:

auto_confirm
driver_approval

Trip lifecycle:

draft
published
boarding
in_progress
completed
cancelled

Keep lifecycle implementation simple for MVP.

Do not create an elaborate workflow state machine yet.

---

15. TRIP CHECKPOINTS

A trip contains an ordered list of checkpoints.

Example:

Alger
  ↓
Blida
  ↓
Médéa
  ↓
Laghouat

Create:

trip_checkpoints

Fields:

id
trip_id
place_id
name
location
sequence
estimated_arrival
created_at

Constraint:

UNIQUE(trip_id, sequence)

A trip requires at least two checkpoints.

The user must be able to:

- add checkpoint
- remove checkpoint
- reorder checkpoint
- select place
- select custom map point

---

16. TRIP SEGMENTS

Automatically derive adjacent route segments.

Example:

Alger → Blida
Blida → Médéa
Médéa → Laghouat

Create:

trip_segments

Fields:

id
trip_id
from_checkpoint_id
to_checkpoint_id
sequence
distance_km
estimated_minutes
reference_price
driver_price
capacity
reserved_seats
created_at

Do not require the driver to manually enter:

distance
ETA

The system calculates them.

---

17. PRICING

Use segment-level pricing.

Each segment has:

reference_price
driver_price

The system calculates a reference price.

The driver may adjust the segment price before publishing.

For a booking:

pickup = checkpoint A
dropoff = checkpoint D

and route:

A → B → C → D

the booking price per seat is:

A-B price
+
B-C price
+
C-D price

At booking creation, snapshot the calculated price.

After confirmation:

«Changing trip segment prices must NOT change the existing booking price.»

Use:

price_per_seat
total_amount

in the booking.

Currency:

DZD

No platform fee in MVP.

---

18. BOOKING

Create:

bookings

Fields:

id
client_id
trip_id
pickup_checkpoint_id
dropoff_checkpoint_id
seats
price_per_seat
total_amount
status
source
created_at
updated_at

Source:

direct
resale

Transfer does NOT need to create a new booking.

A transfer changes the existing booking's trip/checkpoint references while keeping the booking identity and financial snapshot.

---

19. BOOKING STATUS

Keep status simple.

Support the states necessary for:

pending
confirmed
rejected
cancelled
completed
no_show

Do not create unnecessary states unless implementation requires them.

---

20. SEAT RESERVATION

Seat availability must be transactionally safe.

Never implement:

SELECT available seats
INSERT booking

as separate unsafe operations.

Use a PostgreSQL RPC/transaction.

The reservation operation must:

1. Lock the required segment rows.
2. Verify capacity.
3. Verify available seats on every covered segment.
4. Reserve seats atomically.
5. Create/update the booking.
6. Return success/failure.

For a booking:

Alger → Laghouat

reserve seats on:

Alger → Blida
Blida → Médéa
Médéa → Laghouat

This prevents overbooking.

---

21. ONLINE PAYMENT ONLY

This is a critical business rule.

The company NEVER handles cash.

There is:

NO cash payment
NO cash booking
NO cash QR
NO cash debt

All bookings are paid online.

Payment provider must be abstracted.

Create:

booking_payments

with:

id
booking_id
amount
currency
status
provider
provider_reference
paid_at
refunded_amount
created_at
updated_at

Payment status can include:

requires_payment
authorized
paid
failed
expired
refunded
partially_refunded

Support payment retries safely.

Do not assume that a payment provider has been selected yet.

Create an adapter/service interface such as:

interface PaymentProvider {
  createPayment(...)
  authorizePayment(...)
  capturePayment(...)
  refundPayment(...)
  getPaymentStatus(...)
}

---

22. AUTO-CONFIRM BOOKING

For:

booking_mode = auto_confirm

workflow:

Client selects trip
        ↓
System checks seats
        ↓
System creates payment
        ↓
Payment succeeds
        ↓
Booking confirmed

Seat reservation and booking creation must be atomic.

---

23. DRIVER APPROVAL BOOKING

For:

booking_mode = driver_approval

workflow:

Client requests booking
        ↓
Seat reservation
        ↓
Payment authorization / hold
        ↓
Booking pending
        ↓
Driver accepts or rejects

Accept:

authorize
    ↓
capture
    ↓
confirmed

Reject/timeout:

release payment authorization
release seats
booking rejected/expired

Approval timeout must be configurable.

Use a sensible MVP default such as:

30 minutes

but keep it configurable.

---

24. CANCELLATION

Keep cancellation policy intentionally simple.

Eligible client cancellation:

more than 24 hours before trip
AND
more than 30 minutes after booking

Refund:

80%

Otherwise normal cancellation is not eligible for refund.

No-show:

0% refund

Do not build a complicated refund-policy engine.

Use transactional refund logic.

---

25. DRIVER CANCELLATION

Do not implement a complex policy engine.

At MVP level:

- protect confirmed bookings
- prevent unsafe cancellation
- automatically handle pending bookings appropriately
- preserve an audit trail

Detailed exceptional cases can be implemented later through the future graph execution system.

---

26. RESALE

Clients can resell part of an existing booking.

Example:

Original booking:
3 seats

Seller lists:
2 seats

Buyer purchases:
1 seat

Remaining resale quantity:
1 seat

Create:

resale_listings

Fields:

id
booking_id
seller_id
seats_for_sale
seats_sold
price_per_seat
status
created_at
closed_at

Rules:

- Seller specifies seats for sale.
- Buyer may purchase fewer than available.
- Seller cannot sell more than the remaining seats.
- Once all offered seats are sold, listing closes automatically.
- Seller price cannot exceed the original booking's seat price.
- Resale is online-only.
- Buyer receives a normal booking.
- Buyer can later cancel according to normal cancellation rules.
- Buyer can later transfer according to normal transfer rules.

Resale must use atomic seat locking.

---

27. RESALE FINANCIAL MODEL

Example:

Original booking:

3 × 1000 DZD

Seller resells one seat for:

700 DZD

The driver remains entitled to the original trip fare.

The company already holds the original payment.

The resale transaction creates the buyer's new payment and records the resale price.

Do not build a complex accounting engine in the MVP.

Preserve enough data to support later ledger/settlement implementation.

---

28. TRANSFER

Transfer is booking-level.

The whole booking is transferred.

No partial transfer.

Driver initiates transfer.

Workflow:

Driver A
   ↓
requests transfer
   ↓
system finds compatible trips
   ↓
Driver selects target trip
   ↓
Client receives notification
   ↓
Client approves/rejects

Create:

transfer_requests

Fields:

id
booking_id
from_trip_id
to_trip_id
requested_by
status
created_at
responded_at

Status:

pending
approved
rejected
cancelled

During transfer:

seat reservation is protected

If approved:

booking.trip_id

moves to the target trip.

The booking price does NOT change.

Update:

pickup_checkpoint_id
dropoff_checkpoint_id

to compatible checkpoints on the target trip.

Preserve transfer history in:

transfer_requests
audit_logs

Do not create a new booking for a transfer.

---

29. TRANSFER + RESALE RULE

Resale and transfer must not be active simultaneously on the same booking.

Rules:

active transfer
    → cannot create resale

If transfer is cancelled/rejected:

normal resale functionality returns

If a transfer is pending and the client cancels the booking:

transfer request is cancelled

Keep these rules simple.

---

30. SEARCH / ROUTE MATCHING

Client searches:

From
To
Date
Number of seats

A trip matches when:

pickup exists before destination

on the driver's ordered route.

Example trip:

Alger
Blida
Médéa
Laghouat

must match:

Alger → Laghouat
Alger → Médéa
Blida → Laghouat
Blida → Médéa

but not:

Laghouat → Alger
Médéa → Blida

unless another trip direction exists.

Support geographic tolerance around checkpoints using PostGIS.

The search layer should be database-driven and efficient.

Use PostGIS indexes.

---

31. GPS

GPS is only used for:

current location

at this stage.

Do NOT implement continuous user tracking.

A client may use current location when selecting:

From

but can also manually select a place or map point.

---

32. NOTIFICATIONS

Support:

in-app
push

Create:

notifications

Fields:

id
user_id
type
title
body
data
read_at
created_at

Notifications should be generated for important events:

booking request
