---

66. ENVIRONMENT

Provide:

.env.example

with required variables.

Never commit secrets.

Never place private Supabase service-role keys in frontend code.

Frontend may use only the public Supabase key intended for browser use.

---

67. SUPABASE SECURITY

Never expose:

service_role
private API keys
payment provider secrets
admin credentials

in the frontend.

Server-side secrets must remain in:

Supabase Edge Functions secrets

or equivalent secure environment configuration.

---

68. ADMIN BOOTSTRAPPING

The system must support creation of the initial admin through a secure server-side/bootstrap process.

Do not allow public signup to select:

admin

as a freely assignable role.

---

69. DATABASE MIGRATIONS

Use ordered migrations.

Example:

0001_extensions.sql
0002_enums.sql
0003_profiles.sql
0004_verification.sql
0005_places.sql
0006_trips.sql
0007_bookings.sql
0008_payments.sql
0009_resale.sql
0010_transfer.sql
0011_notifications.sql
0012_ratings.sql
0013_settings.sql
0014_audit.sql
0015_rls.sql
0016_functions.sql
0017_seed.sql

The exact migration split may be adjusted, but migrations must be deterministic and repeatable.

---

70. CODE QUALITY

Code must be:

typed
modular
readable
maintainable
consistent
production-oriented

Avoid:

dead code
duplicated logic
giant components
magic numbers
hard-coded business rules
unsafe SQL
client-side authorization

Use constants/configuration for business parameters.

---

71. DO NOT OVERENGINEER

Do NOT implement now:

microservices
complex accounting
driver payroll engine
complex loyalty system
chat
subscriptions
corporate accounts
recurring trips
multi-vehicle drivers
multi-role users
continuous GPS tracking
workflow graph engine
AI recommendation engine
advanced fraud detection

These are future features.

---

72. FINAL ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA

The application is considered complete only when this end-to-end scenario works:

1. Client registers
2. Client uploads identity document
3. Driver registers
4. Driver uploads required documents
5. Admin verifies driver
6. Driver adds vehicle
7. Driver creates trip
8. Driver adds ordered checkpoints
9. System calculates route distance and ETA
10. System generates segments
11. System calculates reference prices
12. Driver adjusts segment prices
13. Driver publishes trip
14. Client searches From/To/date/seats
15. System finds compatible route
16. Client selects trip
17. Client pays online
18. Seats are atomically reserved
19. Booking becomes confirmed
20. Driver sees booking
21. Client sees booking
22. Notifications are generated
23. Client can cancel when eligible
24. Refund is calculated at 80%
25. Client can create resale listing
26. Another client can purchase resale seats
27. Resale listing closes when sold quantity is reached
28. Driver can request booking transfer
29. Client approves transfer
30. Booking moves to compatible trip
31. Booking price remains unchanged
32. Trip completes
33. Client rates driver
34. Driver rates client
35. Admin can inspect the entire lifecycle
36. Audit log records sensitive actions

---

73. AGENT EXECUTION RULES

Work directly on the project.

Do not stop after generating a plan.

Do not provide pseudo-code where real implementation is possible.

When something is technically ambiguous, choose the simplest architecture consistent with the requirements.

Do not invent new business requirements.

Do not silently change existing business rules.

If a technical decision is required, prefer:

simple
transaction-safe
Supabase-native
PostgreSQL-first
mobile-friendly
maintainable

over unnecessary complexity.

Before finishing:

1. Run/build the application.
2. Run database migrations.
3. Validate TypeScript.
4. Validate RLS.
5. Test critical RPCs.
6. Test the booking flow.
7. Test payment-state handling.
8. Test resale.
9. Test transfer.
10. Test responsive UI.
11. Fix discovered errors.
12. Provide a concise implementation summary.
13. Provide exact setup/run/deploy instructions.

The final result must be a real functional MVP, not a conceptual implementation.
